.TH XYMONSETTINGS 8 "Version 4.3.31:  6 Nov 2026" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
xymonsettings.sh \- show and change settings in xymonserver.cfg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymonsettings.sh [\-c FILE] get NAME..."
.br
.B "xymonsettings.sh [\-c FILE] [\-a] set NAME=VALUE..."
.br
.B "xymonsettings.sh [\-c FILE] [\-w DIR] skins"
.br
.B "xymonsettings.sh [\-c FILE] [\-w DIR] skin NAME"
.br
.B "xymonsettings.sh [\-c FILE] [\-w DIR] animation on|off"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonsettings.sh reads and changes the settings in
.I xymonserver.cfg(5)
on an installed Xymon server, without opening the file in an editor,
and switches the web pages between the installed icon sets.

\fBget\fR and \fBset\fR work with a value as a Xymon program sees it. In the
file, a backslash escapes the next character, a "#" outside quotes
starts a comment, and one pair of double quotes around the value is
removed when it is loaded. \fBget\fR undoes that, and \fBset\fR adds the
backslashes a value needs, so a value can hold double quotes, a "#"
or a backslash as it is. A reference to another setting, such as
"$XYMONSERVERWWWURL", is shown and written as it stands: the programs
replace it when they load the file.

Programs read xymonserver.cfg when they start. After a \fBset\fR,
xymongen(1) and the CGI programs use the new value at their next run;
the programs that keep running, such as xymond(8) and its channel
workers, use it once Xymon has been restarted.

.SH COMMANDS
.IP "\fBget\fR NAME..."
Print the value of each NAME, from the last line in the file that
sets it, which is the one the programs use. With one NAME, only the
value is printed; with more, one NAME=VALUE line for each. A NAME
the file does not set is reported on stderr, and the exit status is
then 1.

.IP "\fBset\fR NAME=VALUE..."
Change each NAME to VALUE. Every line that sets NAME is rewritten:
what stands before the name, such as "export ", and the comment
after the value are kept, and every other line of the file stays as
it is. The file is overwritten in place, so its owner, its mode and
a symbolic link pointing to it are kept.

A NAME the file does not have yet is refused, so a misspelt name
does not add a setting that nothing reads; use \fB\-a\fR to add it.
All assignments are checked before the file is changed: when one is
refused, none is applied.

.IP "\fBskins\fR"
List the icon sets ("skins") a \fBskin\fR command can choose, with the
image type of each and "static" when it has a static variant, and
mark the one XYMONSKIN points at with "*", noting when its static
variant is in use.
"classic" is the "gifs" directory in the web directory; every other
name is a directory under "skins" there that has a green status icon.

.IP "\fBskin\fR NAME"
Make the web pages use icon set NAME: set XYMONSKIN to
"$XYMONSERVERWWWURL/gifs" for "classic", or to
"$XYMONSERVERWWWURL/skins/NAME", and set IMAGEFILETYPE to the image
type of its icons, the first of svg, png, gif and jpg that it has a
green.TYPE in. INFOCOLUMNGIF, TRENDSCOLUMNGIF and CLIENTCOLUMNGIF are
set to the skin's info.TYPE, trends.TYPE and clientlog.TYPE, or to
green.TYPE for a column icon the skin does not have. A setting the
file does not have yet is added. When the set in use is its static
variant, NAME's static variant is chosen too; a set without one is
used with its moving icons, and a warning says so.

.IP "\fBanimation\fR on|off"
Make the icons of the set in use move, or stand still: \fBoff\fR points
XYMONSKIN at the set's "static" directory, which has the same icons
standing still, and \fBon\fR back at the set itself. Refused when
XYMONSKIN points at none of the installed sets, or, for \fBoff\fR, when
the set has no static variant.

.SH OPTIONS
.IP "\fB\-c\fR FILE"
The file to read and change. Default: $XYMONHOME/etc/xymonserver.cfg,
the file xymon.sh starts Xymon with.

.IP "\fB\-a\fR"
With \fBset\fR: add a NAME the file does not have yet, as a new line
at the end of the file.

.IP "\fB\-w\fR DIR"
With \fBskins\fR, \fBskin\fR and \fBanimation\fR: the web directory that holds "gifs"
and "skins". Default: XYMONWWWDIR from the settings file, as
xymoncmd(1) expands it.

.SH LIMITATIONS
Only the lines of the file itself are read and changed: a setting
from a file it includes, and the "AREA/NAME=" and "NAME+=" forms,
are left alone. A value cannot hold a newline.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
xymonsettings.sh get XYMONSKIN
xymonsettings.sh set STATUSLIFETIME=60
xymonsettings.sh set XYMONPAGEACKFONT='COLOR="#33ebf4" SIZE="\-1"'
xymonsettings.sh skins
xymonsettings.sh skin accessible
xymonsettings.sh animation off
.fi

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonserver.cfg(5), xymoncmd(1), xymon(7)
